Subject: let a debugger pause code, read variables and evaluate expressions

What this does
  Adds the hooks a debugger needs to work at all:

    - a callback the engine invokes at each statement boundary, so a debugger
      can stop on a line and can tell when it stopped because the script said
      `debugger;`
    - a way to walk the call stack and read the arguments, locals and captured
      variables of any frame on it
    - a way to change one of those variables
    - a way to evaluate an expression as if it were running inside a chosen
      frame, so typing a variable name in a debugger console shows its value

Why we need it
  Without these, no debugger can attach to an app running on this engine.
  There is no way to pause, no way to see a variable, and `debugger;` in the
  source does nothing at all.

  Statement boundaries are only instrumented in code parsed *after* a handler
  is installed, so an app that never attaches a debugger never pays for it.

What it adds
  JS_SetDebugTraceHandler()      - install the per-statement callback.
  JS_GetStackDepth()             - how many frames are on the call stack.
  JS_GetLocalVariablesAtLevel()  - the variables visible in one frame.
  JS_FreeLocalVariables()        - release what the call above returned.
  JS_SetVariableAtLevel()        - change a variable in one frame.
  JS_EvalInStackFrame()          - evaluate an expression inside one frame.

  Two opcodes: a parse-time marker, and the trap the interpreter runs.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes - it adds an opcode. The new opcode goes on the end, so bytecode built
  before this change still loads. Bytecode built after it would confuse an
  older engine, which is what the bump is for. The bump itself is in
  9999-bc-version-bump.

Notes
  The trap opcode is defined whether or not JS_ENABLE_DEBUGGER is set, so that
  opcode numbering never depends on a build flag. With the flag off, only the
  handling is compiled out.

